### 1. Organize the equations

Keep the unknowns in the fixed order $(x,y,z)$. Read the coefficients in that order from each equation, inserting a zero wherever a variable is absent. This gives

$$
\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1\\2&-1&1\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}6\\3\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Equivalently, using the columns of the coefficient matrix, the system says

$$
x\begin{bmatrix}1\\2\end{bmatrix}
+y\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\end{bmatrix}
+z\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
=\begin{bmatrix}6\\3\end{bmatrix}.
$$

Each coordinate of this vector equation reproduces one original equation, so both forms encode exactly the same conditions.
